research/audit/wave7-preserved-refuters/cex-dirichlet-has-no-limit-anywhere--24d97efb59e14ba9.prompt.md-878-
research/audit/wave7-preserved-refuters/cex-dirichlet-has-no-limit-anywhere--24d97efb59e14ba9.prompt.md-879-- **The hypothesis "all terms in $A \setminus \{c\}$" is not decorative.** A sequence allowed to take the value $c$ carries information about $f(c)$, which [[def-function-limit]] deliberately ignores; the constant sequence $z_k = c$ would then refute every limit at once.
research/audit/wave7-preserved-refuters/cex-dirichlet-has-no-limit-anywhere--24d97efb59e14ba9.prompt.md-880-
research/audit/wave7-preserved-refuters/cex-dirichlet-has-no-limit-anywhere--24d97efb59e14ba9.prompt.md:881:- **What the corollary does not say.** It gives a sufficient condition for the limit not to exist, not a necessary one in any weaker form: the converse statement, that a limit exists as soon as all such image sequences converge to one value, is the other direction of [[thm-sequential-criterion-for-function-limits]] and is exactly the direction that spends countable choice ([[rem-heine-criterion-choice-cost]]).
