research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-x-times-dirichlet-has-a-limit-only-at-zero.md-972-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-x-times-dirichlet-has-a-limit-only-at-zero.md-973-**Given:** A set $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}$, a function $f : A \to \mathbb{R}$ and a limit point $c$ of $A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]], [[def-function-limit]]).
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-x-times-dirichlet-has-a-limit-only-at-zero.md-974-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-x-times-dirichlet-has-a-limit-only-at-zero.md:975:[L1] Heine criterion, the direction from the $\varepsilon$-$\delta$ limit to sequences: if $\lim_{x \to c} f(x) = L$ then $f(z_k) \to L$ for every sequence $(z_k)$ with all terms in $A \setminus \{c\}$ converging to $c$ ([[thm-sequential-criterion-for-function-limits]]). That direction is proved without any choice principle.
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-x-times-dirichlet-has-a-limit-only-at-zero.md-976-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-x-times-dirichlet-has-a-limit-only-at-zero.md-977-[L2] A sequence of reals has at most one limit, so two limits of the same sequence are equal, and a sequence with a limit converges ([[lem-limit-unique]], [[def-real-limit]], [[def-sequence]]).
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-x-times-dirichlet-has-a-limit-only-at-zero.md-978-
--
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-x-times-dirichlet-has-a-limit-only-at-zero.md-998-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-x-times-dirichlet-has-a-limit-only-at-zero.md-999-- **The hypothesis "all terms in $A \setminus \{c\}$" is not decorative.** A sequence allowed to take the value $c$ carries information about $f(c)$, which [[def-function-limit]] deliberately ignores; the constant sequence $z_k = c$ would then refute every limit at once.
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-x-times-dirichlet-has-a-limit-only-at-zero.md-1000-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-x-times-dirichlet-has-a-limit-only-at-zero.md:1001:- **What the corollary does not say.** It gives a sufficient condition for the limit not to exist, not a necessary one in any weaker form: the converse statement, that a limit exists as soon as all such image sequences converge to one value, is the other direction of [[thm-sequential-criterion-for-function-limits]] and is exactly the direction that spends countable choice ([[rem-heine-criterion-choice-cost]]).
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-x-times-dirichlet-has-a-limit-only-at-zero.md-1002-````
