research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-sequential-criterion-for-function-limits.prompt.md-875-**Given:** A set $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}$, a function $f : A \to \mathbb{R}$ and a limit point $c$ of $A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]], [[def-function-limit]]).
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-sequential-criterion-for-function-limits.prompt.md-876-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-sequential-criterion-for-function-limits.prompt.md:877:[L1] Heine criterion, the direction from the $\varepsilon$-$\delta$ limit to sequences: if $\lim_{x \to c} f(x) = L$ then $f(z_k) \to L$ for every sequence $(z_k)$ with all terms in $A \setminus \{c\}$ converging to $c$ ([[thm-sequential-criterion-for-function-limits]]). That direction is proved without any choice principle.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-sequential-criterion-for-function-limits.prompt.md-878-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-sequential-criterion-for-function-limits.prompt.md-879-[L2] A sequence of reals has at most one limit, so two limits of the same sequence are equal, and a sequence with a limit converges ([[lem-limit-unique]], [[def-real-limit]], [[def-sequence]]).
