research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-sequential-criterion-for-function-limits.prompt.md-900-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-sequential-criterion-for-function-limits.prompt.md-901-- **The hypothesis "all terms in $A \setminus \{c\}$" is not decorative.** A sequence allowed to take the value $c$ carries information about $f(c)$, which [[def-function-limit]] deliberately ignores; the constant sequence $z_k = c$ would then refute every limit at once.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-sequential-criterion-for-function-limits.prompt.md-902-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-sequential-criterion-for-function-limits.prompt.md:903:- **What the corollary does not say.** It gives a sufficient condition for the limit not to exist, not a necessary one in any weaker form: the converse statement, that a limit exists as soon as all such image sequences converge to one value, is the other direction of [[thm-sequential-criterion-for-function-limits]] and is exactly the direction that spends countable choice ([[rem-heine-criterion-choice-cost]]).
